### Describe the bug

`Microsoft.CodeQuality.Analyzers.QualityGuidelines.DoNotPassDisposablesIntoUnawaitedTasksAnalyzer` (rule CA2025) throws a `NullReferenceException` during compilation, surfacing as `error AD0001` and failing the build.

This is a different trigger shape than the one in #53926 (local async function called with an instance field + a `CancellationTokenSource`). The crash reported here needs all of:

1. Disposables passed into `Task.Factory.StartNew` as a **tuple state object** consumed by a `static` lambda,
2. a second unawaited `Task` created in the same scope from those same underlying objects,
3. a `Task.WhenAny` race between the two tasks, and
4. a later `foreach` disposal of an `IDisposable[]` holding related streams.

Removing any one of those ingredients makes the crash disappear (verified by minimizing the repro below).

### Reproduction

Create a console app (`net10.0`, `<EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>`, `<AnalysisMode>AllEnabledByDefault</AnalysisMode>`) with:

```csharp
using System.IO.Pipelines;

var toServer = new Pipe();
var fromServer = new Pipe();
IDisposable[] streams = [toServer.Writer.AsStream(), fromServer.Reader.AsStream()];

var server = Task.Factory.StartNew(
    static state =>
    {
        var (serverRoot, input, output) = ((string, PipeReader, PipeWriter))state!;
        RunServer(serverRoot, input, output);
    },
    ("root", toServer.Reader, fromServer.Writer),
    CancellationToken.None,
    TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning,
    TaskScheduler.Default);

var connecting = ConnectAsync(toServer.Writer.AsStream(), fromServer.Reader.AsStream());

if (await Task.WhenAny(connecting, server) == server)
{
    await server;
}

await connecting;
foreach (var stream in streams)
{
    stream.Dispose();
}

static async Task<string> ConnectAsync(Stream output, Stream input)
{
    await Task.Yield();
    return "connected";
}

static void RunServer(string root, PipeReader serverInput, PipeWriter serverOutput)
{
    using var input = new StreamReader(serverInput.AsStream());
    using var output = new StreamWriter(serverOutput.AsStream()) { AutoFlush = true };
    Console.WriteLine(root);
}
```

Build it: the output contains

```text
CSC : error AD0001: Analyzer 'Microsoft.CodeQuality.Analyzers.QualityGuidelines.DoNotPassDisposablesIntoUnawaitedTasksAnalyzer' threw an exception of type 'System.NullReferenceException' with message 'Object reference not set to an instance of an object.'.
```

### Affected versions

All verified locally with the SDK-bundled analyzers (no analyzer PackageReference):

- 10.0.400 — crashes
- 10.0.401 (current latest GA) — crashes
- 11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128 — crashes

#53926 was closed by #55904 ("Prevent CA2025 analyzer crash for direct field arguments", merged 2026-09-16, milestone 12.0-preview1). No .NET 10 GA SDK contains that fix, and this shape may not be covered by it in any case.

### Workarounds found

- Restructure the code (we replaced the `Task.Factory.StartNew` + `Task.WhenAny` pair with a dedicated `Thread` + `TaskCompletionSource`, which no longer trips the analyzer).
- `dotnet_diagnostic.CA2025.severity = none` skips the analyzer entirely and prevents the crash (verified: the AD0001 disappears with the entry present and returns when it is removed).

### Question

Can the crash fix be validated against this shape as well, and serviced into the 10.0.4xx band? We hit this in CI on a self-hosted runner whose SDK tracks the current 10.0 GA, and the only repo-side mitigation that keeps the rule surface otherwise intact is disabling CA2025 outright.

